---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'Contact | spousalsupportcalc.com';
const description = 'Contact spousalsupportcalc.com for questions, corrections, or feedback.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Contact</h1>
  <p>Email <a href="mailto:hey@theclicklab.agency">hey@theclicklab.agency</a>. We're a small team, so email is the only channel we monitor &mdash; there's no live chat or phone line, and that's by design: family-law questions deserve a considered written answer, not a rushed one.</p>

  <h2>What we respond to</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Corrections to formulas or statute citations.</strong> If a state's guideline has changed, or you've found a citation that's out of date, tell us which page and what the correct rule is. We verify against the primary source before making any change.</li>
    <li><strong>Errors or unclear language in guides.</strong> If a sentence in a guide is ambiguous, contradicts itself, or is just hard to follow, we want to know &mdash; plain language is one of our core editorial standards.</li>
    <li><strong>Suggestions for new content.</strong> If you searched for a spousal-support question and couldn't find it on the Site &mdash; a state we don't cover yet, a scenario the calculator doesn't handle &mdash; let us know what you were trying to figure out.</li>
    <li><strong>Press and business inquiries.</strong> Journalists working on family-law or divorce-finance stories, and businesses interested in advertising or a data-sharing partnership, can reach the same address.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What to expect</h2>
  <p>We read every message. Corrections and editorial questions typically get a reply within a few business days. If a correction is verified, we update the affected page and note the date of the material change at the bottom of that page, consistent with our <a href="/editorial/">corrections policy</a>. We don't always reply to content suggestions individually, but we do track them for future guides.</p>

  <h2>What we don't respond to</h2>
  <p>We can't evaluate your specific divorce, review your settlement numbers, or tell you what a judge is likely to order in your case &mdash; that requires a licensed family-law attorney who can see your full financial picture and knows your judge and county's local practice. We also don't recommend specific attorneys, mediators, or divorce-tech products, and we don't accept payment to feature or promote anyone on the Site. If you email us asking for case-specific advice, we'll point you back to this policy and suggest contacting a family-law attorney in your state.</p>

  <h2>Before you email</h2>
  <p>Many questions we get are already answered on the Site: check the <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a> for common questions on duration, taxes, modification, and remarriage, or the <a href="/guides/">guides</a> for state-specific rules and dispute walkthroughs. If your question is about how the calculator arrived at a number, the <a href="/methodology/">methodology page</a> walks through the formula and worked examples in detail.</p>

  <h2>If you actually need an attorney</h2>
  <p>We don't make referrals, but every state bar association runs a lawyer-referral service that will connect you with a licensed family-law attorney in your area, often for a low-cost initial consultation. Many states also fund legal-aid organizations that handle spousal-support and divorce matters for people below a certain income threshold, and most county courthouses have a self-help or family-law facilitator's office that can help pro se litigants with forms and procedure even if they can't give legal advice. Searching "[your state] bar association lawyer referral service" or "[your county] family law facilitator" is usually the fastest way to find the right office.</p>
</BaseLayout>
